\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$, and $\mathbb Z,\mathbb Q,\mathbb R,\mathbb C$ denote the integers, rationals, reals and complex numbers. Any other conventions are specified locally.


For a finite group $G$, conjugacy is the relation $x\sim y$ if $y=g^{-1}xg$ for some $g\in G$. Let $k(G)$ be the number of its conjugacy classes, including the identity class. Odd order means that $|G|$ is odd; nonabelian means that some pair of elements fails to commute.
\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Does there exist a finite nonabelian group $G$ of odd order satisfying
\[
 \frac{k(G)}{|G|}=\frac1{17}?
\]
The ratio is the exact rational number $1/17$, rather than an asymptotic density or approximation. \href{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}{[S2]}

\subsection{Short English statement}
Is there a finite nonabelian odd-order group whose conjugacy-class count is exactly one seventeenth of its order?

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{description}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, \emph{UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems}, record 2597 (KOU-21.88). CC BY 4.0. \url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
\item[S2] D. MacHale, Problem 21.88, in E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), \emph{The Kourovka Notebook: Unsolved Problems in Group Theory}, 21st edition, arXiv:1401.0300v48 (6 October 2026). This version records a negative answer; the displayed question is the historically registered target. \url{https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v48}.
\end{description}
